<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sapaudia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sapaudia was a late Roman name for a region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 443 the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> resettled the surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> there, and from this base they built a kingdom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley. The name is the origin of Savoy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sapaudia.jpg" alt="Sapaudia" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marco Zanoli (Sidonius) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Koenigreich_Burgund_DE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Sapaudia, also written Sabaudia, is a Latinisation of local words for forest or upland forest, though it is often glossed as fir, from the roughly similar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sapinus. It developed into Saboia and from there into the Italian Savoia and the French Savoie. The 4th-century Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> is the first to mention it, as the southern district of Provincia Maxima Sequanorum, the land of the Sequani as enlarged by the Diocletian Reforms. Its prefect appears in the late Roman List of Offices.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 5th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> settled in the area and formed the Kingdom of the Burgundians, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, and for centuries afterwards the names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Sapaudia, or Savoy, remained closely linked. In the mid-9th century Sapaudia was ruled by the Bosonid duke Hucbert as part of the realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a>, and in 933 it was incorporated into the Kingdom of Burgundy of Rudolph II.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy">Rudolph III of Burgundy</a> made Humbert I count over the area. For supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry II, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, Humbert also received the secular powers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta">Aosta</a> after the death of Bishop Anselm. When Rudolph III died in 1032, Humbert accepted the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Conrad II, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and helped him put down the revolts of Count Odo and Burchard. He was rewarded with the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a>, carved out of the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, and lands in Chablais and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentaise-valley">Tarentaise Valley</a>, taken from the diocese of Tarentaise at Moûtiers. This enlarged territory became known as the county of Savoy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sapaudia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 443 the survivors were resettled in <b>Sapaudia</b>, around Geneva.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-burgundians">Kingdom of the Burgundians</a></li></ul></section>
